✅ Integration tests (echo server)\n- ✅ Load tests (k6 with 100 connections)\n- ✅ E2E tests (full connection flow)\n\nThis is **comprehensive and appropriate**.\n\n### Migration Path - Validated\n\nThe plan correctly identifies:\n1. No breaking changes to existing workspaces\n2. New workspaces can choose SSM or WebSocket\n3. Gradual migration possible\n4. Rollback trivial (just change `createConnectionHandler`)\n\n## Final Assessment\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Architecturally Sound**: Factory pattern, interface-based design, separation of concerns\n2. **Minimal Changes**: Surgical modifications to existing code\n3. **Backward Compatible**: Zero impact on existing SSM workspaces\n4. **Well-Researched**: Correctly identifies all existing components to reuse\n5. **Production-Ready**: Includes metrics, logging, health checks, graceful shutdown\n6. **Testable**: Clear testing strategy with unit, integration, and load tests\n\n### Weaknesses (Minor)\n\n1. **Request Context**: Needs clarification on passing user to handler\n2. **Documentation**: Some code examples have minor errors\n3. **Operational Details**: DNS/TLS setup not fully specified\n\n### Recommendation\n\n**PROCEED WITH IMPLEMENTATION** with these adjustments:\n\n1. **Fix user context passing** in handler interface\n2. **Add token type constant** to jwt package\n3. **Document operational setup** (DNS, TLS, Traefik middleware)\n4. **Create POC first** (as plan suggests) to validate assumptions\n\nThe plan is **well-thought-out, technically sound, and ready for implementation**. The architecture leverages existing infrastructure correctly, follows best practices, and maintains backward compatibility. The proposed WebSocket proxy is lightweight, efficient, and production-ready."
